From 5a2b46e7e874093b5424f8e2c3d0629026e5a743 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlanFokCo <alanfok2868@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 13:50:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] aiprof: bound the CUPTI teardown so a wedged driver still
 reports

CuptiSink::Stop() called cuptiActivityDisable() and cuptiActivityFlushAll()
inline on the duration thread. Both can wedge inside the driver: gdb on a
hung target shows Stop() blocked in pthread_rwlock_wrlock inside libcuda
under cuptiActivityDisable, the application's own thread blocked in
pthread_rwlock_wrlock under cuLaunchKernel on a different lock, and CUPTI's
worker threads parked in sem_wait. Measured on a 4x A10 host with driver
580.126.09 against a continuously launching CUDA workload, roughly a third
of collection windows hung there.

The consequences were worse than the hang itself. Stop() never reached
WriteChromeTrace, so no trace file appeared, and it never reached either
terminal Notify, so an orchestrator waiting for CUPTIProfilingWriterOver got
nothing and fell back to its own idle watchdog. CollectionFramework's
watchdog then exits 0, so a window that had collected nothing reported
success.

Run the teardown under a deadline instead, and on timeout report the window
as failed: keep whatever CUPTI had already delivered, write it out, say so
on stderr, and send CUPTIProfilingFailed, the terminal message that replaces
WriterOver. In the observed wedge the hang is at the first
cuptiActivityDisable, before any buffer was flushed, so that file is an
empty trace and the gain is not the data. It is that the window ends in
37s with a terminal message and the collector marked failed, instead
of running out the orchestrator's idle budget in silence. A partial trace
plus an explicit failure beats no file, no message and a successful exit.

docs/embedding.md defined CUPTIProfilingFailed as "the output file could not
be written; this window produces no file" and told consumers not to wait for
a file, which the above makes untrue, so the table row and the paragraph
after it now say the window did not complete and that a file may be absent
or may hold a partial window.

The bound is 30s by default, with CUPROF_TEARDOWN_TIMEOUT_MS as an override
clamped to 1000..600000 ms. It is a wedge detector rather than an allowance for
slow teardowns, and it is flat rather than derived from the window length: the
heaviest teardown measured on that host, a 20s window that collected 4475153
events into a 1.24 GB trace, took 82 ms of disable plus flush, so the default is
about 370x the worst healthy case observed. The residual the teardown drains
does not grow with the window either, because CUPTI delivers buffers continuously
while it runs. What does grow with the window is the WriteChromeTrace that
follows, 7.8s for that same trace, and this bound does not cover it. That limits
what the bound buys: CollectionFramework waits duration + 60s for a terminal
message, and on a long enough window the write alone can exceed that slack,
patched or not.

The deadline primitive lives in a new bounded_call.{h,cc} rather than in
cupti_sink.cc because it has no CUDA or CUPTI dependency, which lets
test/native cover it on any host with a C++11 compiler. That matters: the
primitive is what the whole fix rests on, and a body that never returns is
exactly the shape of the real failure. On timeout the worker thread and its
state are leaked deliberately, since a thread stuck inside the driver cannot
be cancelled without risking the target's address space. The wait is on
CLOCK_MONOTONIC wherever pthread_condattr_setclock exists, so a wall clock
step can neither collapse the bound nor stretch it.

CUPTI's activity calls are process-global, so moving them onto another
thread does not change what they observe. A flush that completes after the
timeout appends to an events_ vector that Stop() has already swapped empty,
and those records belong to a window that was reported failed, so Start()
now clears events_ before enabling anything. Without that they would be
written into the next window's trace and announced as a normal WriterOver,
which is a wrong result rather than a lost one.

For the same reason a timed-out teardown retires the instance: Start()
afterwards fails fast with its own CUPTIProfilingFailed rather than collecting
a window that the abandoned teardown can still disable from underneath it, or
that wedges in cuptiActivityEnable in turn. The thread is still in the driver
holding locks nothing here can see, so the state is not recoverable from inside
the library. To be precise about who reaches that second Start():
CollectionFramework does not, because CUPTIPluginWrapper::custom_trigger
declines to trigger a pid whose state is already past InitSuccess, even though
its scheduler answers CollectFailed by starting the next window for that pid.
The path being guarded is cuprof's own documented one, docs/embedding.md step 4
and CuptiSink::Start()'s contract, under which one loaded instance serves
consecutive windows and the embedder calls cuprof_start() again. A library
cannot assume its embedder declines the second call, and InitializeInjection()'s
return value is discarded by both the CUDA driver and CollectionFramework's
injector, so a silent refusal would leave that window unreported.
